WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.759
Thanks to Paul Bettany's portrayal of the
character in the Marvel Cinematic Universe,

00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:09.050
Vision is now known to the moviegoing masses
as a full-fledged member of the Avengers — but

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:13.260
the super-powerful synthezoid hasn't always
been a friend to Earth's Mightiest Heroes.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:17.660
Since his introduction in the late '60s, Vision
has been the focus of some truly memorable

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:18.160
arcs.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.920
From his undeniable similarities to a certain
DC character, to his penchant for taking over

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.880
the planet, here's everything Marvel doesn't
care for you to know about Vision.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.060
Not that Vision

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:32.099
The version of the Vision made popular by
the MCU has been around in the comics for

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.980
several decades now, but he wasn't actually
the first character to use the name.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:41.060
In November 1940, four weeks before Captain
America made his debut, an alien lawkeeper

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:45.120
by the name of Aarkus was introduced to the
Marvel Universe, appearing through a smoky

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.560
portal in the pages of Marvel Mystery Comics
#13.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:53.360
After freezing a mobster alive, he introduced
himself as "The Vision" and explained that

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.010
he'd come to Earth to fight crime.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.030
His vigilante spree didn't last very long,
with the character making his last appearance

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:01.140
for some time in 1943.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:06.600
In 1968, Marvel scribe Roy Thomas approached
Stan Lee about adding Vision to the Avengers

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:07.100
lineup.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.720
He'd grown frustrated at not being able to
use Marvel's heavy hitters, and wanted to

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.220
bolster the ranks with the neglected alien
hero.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.240
When Lee said no, Thomas sort of went ahead
and just did it anyway, using Aarkus as an

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.340
inspiration for a character with a strikingly
familiar name and look.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.780
Thomas spoke to the magazine Back Issue in
2014, saying,

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.280
"'[Stan] wanted the new Avenger to be an android,
though he never said why.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:32.409
[...] I split the difference and called the
android the Vision and adapted the old costume,

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:34.000
but little else, for the new character."

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.600
Red Tornado watch

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.120
The similarities Vision has with Aarkus were
intentional.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.479
The things he has in common with DC's Red
Tornado?

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:42.920
That's a different story.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.500
If you aren't familiar, Red Tornado made his
debut appearance in Justice League of America

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:53.820
#64 in August 1968, two months before Marvel
introduced the new Vision to Avengers readers.

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:58.440
In the issue, the Justice Society of America
is shocked when a robotic version of the hero

00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:02.049
they once knew as a different Red Tornado
arrives at their headquarters.

00:02:02.049 --> 00:02:06.400
At first, they're skeptical of his motives,
but a mutual trust emerges as they realize

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.100
the big red guy is on their side.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:11.420
The parallels between the Red Tornado and
Vision were striking.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:16.220
Both had red skin and a high-collared costume,
and both were created by a supervillain to

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.340
destroy a team of heroes that they would promptly
end up joining.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:24.240
Even weirder, both characters were android-inspired
reworkings of older comic book characters

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.060
of the same name.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.280
We'll never be 100 percent certain which of
the characters was created first.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.600
For whatever reason, robots with emotions
was one of 1968's weird vibes.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:34.980
Double Vision

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.420
While the Aarkus version of Vision may have
inspired the Vision we know, the original

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:40.440
didn't stay gone.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:44.840
In the early '90s, Roy Thomas got his wish
and was finally given permission to bring

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:46.600
Aarkus back into the Marvel Universe.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.770
The Golden Age alien returned to action in
the third issue of The Invaders, coming back

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.980
to do what he does best — fight Nazis.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.410
The original Vision continues to pop up from
time to time in modern Marvel comics, helping

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.160
out his allies from the Golden Age whenever
they need a hand.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.840
Confusingly, he's often still called Vision
instead of Aarkus.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.940
Even by comic book standards, that makes things
a little complicated.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.080
"I suppose we're both disappointments."

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:10.660
Logic and emotion

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:15.260
While Vision comes off as one of the most
original of the on-screen Avengers, his original

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:18.260
creation was heavily informed by contemporary
sci-fi.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.400
According to Thomas, Vision's personality
and temperament was inspired by the character

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.880
of Spock, the half-human, half-Vulcan first
officer of the USS Enterprise.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.220
Despite their superficial differences, both
characters serve the same function — they're

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:35.610
wells of knowledge that, in spite of their
outward coldness, have deep wells of compassion

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.220
and long for acceptance.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:42.760
Another inspiration for Vision was "I, Robot",
a 1939 short story by Otto and Earl Binder

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.480
With that story, which pre-dated Isaac Asimov's
version by decades, the brothers introduced

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:52.140
the character of Adam Link, a self-aware robot
that Thomas calls "one of the first sympathetic

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:53.080
robot stories."

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:58.230
Over a series of ten tales, Adam went to war,
participated in sports, was put on trial,

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.980
and fought to be recognized as a sentient
citizen.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.060
His arc helped paved the way for countless
sci-fi stories that followed, from Age of

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.230
Ultron to, well, I, Robot.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.330
"Robots don't feel fear.

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:10.780
They don't feel anything.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:13.620
They don't get hungry they don't sleep."

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:14.340
"I do.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.160
I have even had dreams."

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:18.500
Vanishing act

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.660
Filmgoers already know the adorable romance
between Vision and Scarlet Witch.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.690
They're two outsiders with a romantic connection,
and it's a relationship with roots in the

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:29.980
comics, making them much more than just Avengers
with benefits.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.280
Taking some time off from saving the world,
the comic book couple married, moved to the

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.300
suburbs, and most puzzlingly, made twins together.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:41.040
That's right, and Doctor Strange even delivered
the babies — seriously.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:41.680
But how?

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.880
Turns out, when superpowers are involved,
life finds a way.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.960
Scarlet Witch's pregnancy came about through
a combination of her probability-altering

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.220
hex powers and — literally — "magick".

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:55.620
The fact that Vision and Scarlet Witch were
able to conceive at all was a little far-fetched,

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:59.300
even for comics, and their domestic bliss
quickly turned to psychological horror.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:03.960
As it turned out, little Tommy and Billy weren't
real children, but magical/demonic hybrid

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.180
constructs fueled by Scarlet Witch's powerful
subconscious.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:12.200
The ordeal, somewhat understandably, temporarily
turned Scarlet Witch completely insane, and

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.860
her marriage to Vision deteriorated in the
aftermath.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:19.180
The two separated and started operating on
different Avengers teams, the clearest form

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:20.770
of superhero divorce there is.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:24.770
While the event tore the young family apart,
there was some semblance of a happy ending

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:29.490
years later when Young Avengers introduced
the mutants Wiccan and Speed, reincarnations

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:32.140
of the twins who would eventually meet their
mom for real.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:33.680
Visions of evil

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:37.380
Vision's absurd power set makes him a formidable
hero to contend with.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:42.380
Able to fly, shoot lasers, control technology,
shapeshift, phase through solid matter, heal

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:43.699
himself and more.

00:05:43.699 --> 00:05:46.850
He's the sort of character who could probably
take over the world if he wanted.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:51.460
So in classic What If? fashion, Marvel's writers
have occasionally had him do just that.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:58.180
In 1985's Mighty Avengers #253, Vision abandoned
his physical form so he could assume control

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:02.100
of the world's entire computer network and
create a new paradise on Earth.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.640
The Avengers had started to become wary of
his odd behavior over the previous few issues,

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:09.310
and were forced to stop him before he got
too powerful.

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:13.180
Vision's actions led to the synthezoid being
dismantled by government officials, but he

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:14.880
wouldn't stay bits and pieces forever.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:18.960
Vision has also been teased as a powerful
monster, like when he was recently compelled

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.949
to join up with the Hydra Avengers, the team
of heroes-gone-bad that was featured in the

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:25.800
2017 crossover event Secret Empire.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:30.460
Under the control of an A.I. virus, Vision
helped a Hydra-aligned Captain America as

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:34.400
he set out on a mission to take over the world
on the evil organization's behalf.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:38.820
It's a scary situation for the heroes when
Vision turns bad, and hard enough to deal

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:40.280
with that it doesn't happen often.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:44.740
Fortunately, Paul Bettany's Vision seems much
too purehearted for that — unless, of course,

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:46.480
your name is Hawkeye or War Machine.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:48.020
Thanks for watching!

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:50.889
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:50.889 --> 00:06:53.210
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

